WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.480
If you just finished watching Netflix's new&nbsp;
animated film "The Witcher: Nightmare of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.160
Wolf," you might be a little confused,&nbsp;
even if you're a fan of the franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.560
So let's explore some of the biggest unanswered&nbsp;
questions posed by the animated spin-off.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.320
If all of "The Witcher" you've consumed&nbsp;
has been its most popular entries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.920
"The Witcher III: The Wild Hunt"&nbsp;
and the 2019 Netflix show, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.160
likely to have some questions about a name&nbsp;
often alluded to but seldom expanded upon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.000
Vesemir. Geralt's old teacher in "The&nbsp;
Witcher" video games, Vesemir has his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.160
own tragic origin story, which is the main&nbsp;
focus of "The Witcher: Nightmare of the Wolf."

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.880
Vesemir spent his childhood as a common "street&nbsp;
rat," stealing food and coin in order to get by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.520
while also serving a rich master as a servant.&nbsp;
When a witcher named Deglan comes to exorcise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.760
a monster possessing a woman, Vesemir gets&nbsp;
a taste of the action and thirsts for more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.640
offering himself as payment as part of the Law&nbsp;
of Surprise, a custom central to "Witcher" lore.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:59.120
"Lioness of Cintra! I come to claim what is&nbsp;
rightfully mine! Pavetta. By the Law of Surprise."

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.000
Skipping way ahead to the end of this particular&nbsp;
adventure, Vesemir is saddled with the care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.400
of the remaining witchers-to-be, with most&nbsp;
of the remaining witchers missing or dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.560
There are so many questions related to this&nbsp;
cataclysmic event, but the biggest ones are:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.560
What will happen to Vesemir as fate forces him&nbsp;
to become the teacher he doesn't want to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.560
and how will that affect the boys in his care?

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.840
There's a lot of mystery and intrigue surrounding&nbsp;
all of these characters, Lady Zerbst perhaps most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.000
of all. She was a childhood friend of Vesemir's&nbsp;
until he decided to undergo the brutal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.680
deadly training of becoming a witcher,&nbsp;
leaving her behind. She did not, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.080
share Vesemir's humble status. She's of noble&nbsp;
blood, and rose through the ranks due to careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.800
political ploys and "lucky" coincidences to&nbsp;
eventually become an esteemed advisor to the king.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.880
After the events of "Nightmare of the Wolf,"&nbsp;
however, Tetra uses a powerful illusion to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.200
trick Vesemir into fatally wounding Lady&nbsp;
Zerbst, who is now a 70-year-old woman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.400
Vesemir, who is of a similar age&nbsp;
but much younger-looking due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.160
expanded lifespan gifted to witchers,&nbsp;
tearfully says goodbye to Lady Zerbst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.080
next to the lake they dreamed of sharing&nbsp;
together, as she dies in his arms, at peace.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.680
This might seem to be a definite end&nbsp;
to Lady Zerbst's story, but then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.280
she may appear as a ghost, wraith,&nbsp;
or other creature of the underworld.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.120
Or we may get to go back in time and&nbsp;
see more of her story. Just who exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.200
is Lady Zerbst, and how innocent is&nbsp;
she really? We may never fully know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.720
but we sure hope some piece of "Witcher"&nbsp;
media expands on this woman's background.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.360
Apparently named after the traditional&nbsp;
Japanese onomatopoeia for a fox's cry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.720
Kitsu is an unnatural mutation of many mysterious&nbsp;
beings, but often appears as a deceptively cute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.240
fox. She also, however, appears as a tree&nbsp;
spirit, a basilisk and perhaps even an elf.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.880
Like a fox, Kitsu is one sly creature known for&nbsp;
playing tricks, so it's impossible to fully know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.600
what she is or isn't capable of. However, we do&nbsp;
believe she was created by Deglan in a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.480
of unnatural, unholy and unethical experiments&nbsp;
on various creatures, most of which died in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.360
process, but a small number of whom survived and&nbsp;
escaped. Kitsu appears to be one such creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.440
who lashes out at humanity with a power&nbsp;
she struggles to control. Despite all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.600
she is a fundamentally well-meaning creature&nbsp;
who earns Vesemir's sympathy and mercy.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.280
Where is she now? We can only guess,&nbsp;
but we wouldn't be surprised to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.360
more of Kitsu in the future, perhaps even&nbsp;
in Netflix's second season of "The Witcher."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.320
It would be quite the showdown&nbsp;
if she got on Geralt's bad side.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.360
Deglan grew up in the Skellige Isles, where he&nbsp;
was taught the worst lessons from a young age:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.920
Never show fear or mercy; rule with an iron&nbsp;
fist; think of your cause first, yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.280
second, and everyone else dead last. He's the&nbsp;
franchise's walking totem of toxic masculinity.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.720
Like most characters in the universe of "The&nbsp;
Witcher," however, he's still relatable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.160
even if he is the closest thing to a&nbsp;
villain in the movie. As a witcher himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.920
he inspired Vesemir to take up the life he&nbsp;
chooses, and trained him to become one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.080
the strongest witchers ever -- even if the&nbsp;
process nearly killed him. He is the morally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.800
gray father figure to Vesemir as Vesemir will&nbsp;
become the morally gray father figure to Geralt.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.280
However, he crosses the line when he creates&nbsp;
the demented, tortured, monstrous mutants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.920
including Kitsu. He intends to sic these&nbsp;
tortured creations on the unsuspecting humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.000
who would then have no choice but to provide&nbsp;
more coin to the witchers in order to eradicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.920
them. It's an ingenious, evil plan, until Vesemir&nbsp;
puts a stop to it, killing Deglan in the process.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.920
Barring any spiritual hauntings or miraculous&nbsp;
recoveries, this appears to be the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.600
the tale of Deglan, but we still have&nbsp;
much to learn about his own origins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.240
Will we ever learn the exact chilling details of&nbsp;
what made Deglan become such a twisted person?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.560
We aren't holding our breath, but one can dream.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.960
After everything, however, "Nightmare of&nbsp;
the Wolf" has one last ace up its sleeve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.960
The seemingly inconsequential bald kid,&nbsp;
who runs away from Kaer Morhen along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.240
the other boys too young to fight,&nbsp;
is actually none other than Geralt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.720
the famous witcher who acts as the main character&nbsp;
of both the Netflix series and the video games.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.360
Alongside Geralt appear to be Remus, Tomas and&nbsp;
Luka. It's a lot of names to keep track of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.600
but we can at least guess where some of his&nbsp;
friends end up. Remus appears in the Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.600
live-action series as a freelance witcher, so&nbsp;
you can find out what happens to him there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.520
We couldn't find much on Tomas, suggesting&nbsp;
that he may have died as a common casualty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.240
of the witcher-creating process. As for Luka, the&nbsp;
official Netflix "Witcher" page about him says he,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.040
quote, "realized early on that the witcher life&nbsp;
wasn't for him," so it sounds like he's retired.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.520
Of course, Geralt's story is always being shaped&nbsp;
and twisted by whoever gets the privilege to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.600
it. The next big chapter, however, will be&nbsp;
the second season of Netflix's series "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.920
Witcher," which debuts exclusively on&nbsp;
the streaming platform December 17th.

